From aae0b3894e62715ef3627d1dd07b46f17b613873 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Juan J. Sanchez" <jjsanchez@igalia.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 10:52:59 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [GTK][WPE] Give password managers the login fields of pages
 Need the bug URL (OOPS!).

Reviewed by NOBODY (OOPS!).

Before this change, a GLib application that wanted to offer saved
passwords had to load its own code into the web process to find the
login fields of a page. WebKit deprecated that way, since it does not
fit site isolation, which puts frames of other sites in processes of
their own.

With this change, WebKit finds the fields itself with AutofillElements,
the code that Cocoa already uses for this, and tells the application
which login form has the focus, which site it belongs to, and what the
page submits. The application can then offer the saved logins of the
site, fill the form when the user picks one, and offer to save a new
login after a sign in. A field keeps its form while the view does not
have the focus, since password managers show their list of logins, or
ask to unlock them, in windows of their own. A password field that the
page turned into a text field, as to show the password, still counts as
one, so that it keeps its form and a sign in with it shown is reported.

The site of a form comes from what the UI process knows of its frame,
not from the web process, so that a compromised web process cannot claim
another site and get its logins offered. Only http and https documents
get a site, so that local files, which all share one origin, do not
share saved logins. WebKit fills only while the field that had the focus
still has it, so that a login never lands in another document, or in
fields that the page swapped in, after the user picked it. The feature
is off by default, so that applications that do not use it never
receive the passwords that users type.

